{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'","aggregated_output":"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '235,340p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc | sed -n '235,350p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc | sed -n '165,380p'\"","aggregated_output":"   235\t                                     (assoc store\n   236\t                                            cursor-id\n   237\t                                            (assoc cursor-data\n   238\t                                                   :cursor\n   239\t                                                   (:dao.stream/cursor\n   240\t                                                     result))))})]\n   241\t        (case o\n   242\t          :dao.stream/ok (advance state (:dao.stream/value result))\n   243\t          :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   244\t                               :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   245\t                               :stream-id stream-id,\n   246\t                               :state state}\n   247\t          :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :state state}\n   248\t          :dao.stream/gap (advance state :dao.stream/gap)\n   249\t          (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   250\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id, :cursor-id cursor-id}))))))\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\t(defn handle-close\n   254\t  \"Handle :stream/close. `close!` is total over {ok} and wakes nothing: a\n   255\t   reader parked on this stream learns of the close from its own next `next`.\n   256\t   Returns {:state s'}.\"\n   257\t  [state effect]\n   258\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   259\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   260\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   261\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   262\t    (stream/close! handle)\n   263\t    {:state state}))\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\t(defn- augment-wait-entry\n   267\t  \"Resolve one wait entry to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   268\t   store. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an earlier round\n   269\t   baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same cursor-ref may\n   270\t   have advanced the stored cursor since.\"\n   271\t  [store entry]\n   272\t  (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n   273\t    (let [cursor-data (get store (:id cursor-ref))]\n   274\t      (assoc entry\n   275\t             :stream (or (:stream entry)\n   276\t                         (get store (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n   277\t             :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)))\n   278\t    (if (and (not (:stream entry)) (:stream-id entry))\n   279\t      (assoc entry :stream (get store (:stream-id entry)))\n   280\t      entry)))\n   281\t\n   282\t\n   283\t(defn- poll-wait-entry\n   284\t  \"Poll one resolved wait entry against its transport, synchronously.\n   285\t\n   286\t   Returns nil while the entry must keep waiting — `blocked` for a reader,\n   287\t   `full` for a writer, the only two outcomes that can change on their own —\n   288\t   and otherwise the ready-entry updates `{:value :status}`, plus `:cursor`\n   289\t   for a reader that moved. `end` and `gap` resolve to the values the\n   290\t   immediate path yields; any other outcome resolves under its own keyword\n   291\t   for `resume-from-run-queue` to raise. A parked writer retries by\n   292\t   appending: the append is an effect of this poll. An entry with no known\n   293\t   `:reason` keeps waiting.\"\n   294\t  [entry]\n   295\t  (case (:reason entry)\n   296\t    :next (let [result (stream/next (:stream entry) (:cursor entry))\n   297\t                o (outcome result)]\n   298\t            (case o\n   299\t              :dao.stream/ok {:value (:dao.stream/value result),\n   300\t                              :status :ok,\n   301\t                              :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   302\t              :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   303\t              :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :status :end}\n   304\t              :dao.stream/gap {:value :dao.stream/gap,\n   305\t                               :status :dao.stream/gap,\n   306\t                               :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   307\t              {:value o, :status o}))\n   308\t    :put (let [o (outcome (stream/append! (:stream entry) (:datom entry)))]\n   309\t           (case o\n   310\t             :dao.stream/ok {:value (:datom entry), :status :ok}\n   311\t             :dao.stream/full nil\n   312\t             {:value o, :status o}))\n   313\t    nil))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn check-wait-set\n   317\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n   318\t\n   319\t   Every entry is resolved to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   320\t   store and polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution\n   321\t   happens per poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle.\n   322\t   There is no transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only\n   323\t   mechanism.\n   324\t\n   325\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   326\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   327\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values — the\n   328\t   value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next — instead\n   329\t   of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll cursor. An entry\n   330\t   that stays waiting is retained in its stored, resource-id form; the\n   331\t   resolved copy was for this poll only.\"\n   332\t  [state]\n   333\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   334\t    (if (empty? wait-set)\n   335\t      state\n   336\t      (loop [remaining wait-set\n   337\t             store (:store state)\n   338\t             waiting []\n   339\t             woken []]\n   340\t        (if (empty? remaining)\n   235\t                            (stream/cursor source\n   236\t                                           (or (:forward-anchor (:config serving))\n   237\t                                               stream/anchor-oldest)))\n   238\t            session (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome source-cursor))\n   239\t                      (traffic-session offer (make-traffic offer)))]\n   240\t        (if-not session\n   241\t          (call-close! close-attachment! socket-handle)\n   242\t          (let [ack {:ws/attachment attachment\n   243\t                     :ws/command :ws/accept\n   244\t                     :ws/deposit (:traffic session)\n   245\t                     :ws/admission (:admission session)}\n   246\t                ack-writer (get-in (:config serving) [:slots slot-index :ack-writer])\n   247\t                appended (stream/append! (:dao.stream/handle ack-writer) ack)]\n   248\t            (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome appended))\n   249\t              (swap! (:state serving) assoc-in\n   250\t                     [:sessions attachment]\n   251\t                     (assoc session\n   252\t                            :path (:ws/path descriptor)\n   253\t                            :identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)\n   254\t                            :source source\n   255\t                            :forward-state (forward/initial-state\n   256\t                                             (:dao.stream/cursor source-cursor))))\n   257\t              (call-close! close-attachment! socket-handle))))))))\n   258\t\n   259\t\n   260\t(defn- poll-offers!\n   261\t  [serving]\n   262\t  (doseq [[index slot] (map-indexed vector (:slots (:config serving)))]\n   263\t    (let [cursor (get-in (state serving) [:offer-cursors index])\n   264\t          reader (target-handle (:offer-reader slot))\n   265\t          result (stream/next reader cursor)]\n   266\t      (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   267\t        (swap! (:state serving) assoc-in [:offer-cursors index]\n   268\t               (:dao.stream/cursor result))\n   269\t        (acknowledge-offer! serving index (:dao.stream/value result))))))\n   270\t\n   271\t\n   272\t(defn- poll-control!\n   273\t  [serving]\n   274\t  (let [{:keys [control-reader]} (:config serving)\n   275\t        result (stream/next (target-handle control-reader)\n   276\t                            (:control-cursor (state serving)))]\n   277\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   278\t      (swap! (:state serving) assoc :control-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result))\n   279\t      (let [event (:dao.stream/value result)]\n   280\t        ;; Only pre-accept lifecycle belongs in control.  An established\n   281\t        ;; attachment's lifecycle is observed through its own traffic medium.\n   282\t        (when (terminal-event? event)\n   283\t          (remove-session! serving (:ws/attachment event)))))))\n   284\t\n   285\t\n   286\t(defn- poll-traffic!\n   287\t  [serving attachment session]\n   288\t  (let [result (stream/next (:traffic-reader session) (:traffic-cursor session))]\n   289\t    (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   290\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   291\t      (let [event (:dao.stream/value result)]\n   292\t        (if (terminal-event? event)\n   293\t          (remove-session! serving attachment)\n   294\t          (do\n   295\t            (swap! (:state serving) assoc-in [:sessions attachment :traffic-cursor]\n   296\t                   (:dao.stream/cursor result))\n   297\t            ;; This is an interpreter call made by the driver, never a host\n   298\t            ;; callback.  With no interpreter, requests stay ordinary traffic\n   299\t            ;; data and are intentionally not appended to the served stream.\n   300\t            (when-let [interpret (:inbound-step (:config serving))]\n   301\t              (interpret session event)))))\n   302\t\n   303\t      :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   304\t\n   305\t      ;; A traffic gap or terminal reader failure means this composition can no\n   306\t      ;; longer faithfully associate inbound events with the attachment.\n   307\t      (close-session! serving attachment))))\n   308\t\n   309\t\n   310\t(defn- advance-forwarder!\n   311\t  [serving attachment session]\n   312\t  (let [result (forward/forward-step (:source session)\n   313\t                                     (:socket-handle session)\n   314\t                                     (:forward-state session)\n   315\t                                     (:forward-options (:config serving)))]\n   316\t    (case (:status result)\n   317\t      (:continue :retry)\n   318\t      (swap! (:state serving) assoc-in [:sessions attachment :forward-state] result)\n   319\t\n   320\t      :source-ended\n   321\t      (do\n   322\t        ;; Generic close! is only detachment.  This injected policy is the\n   323\t        ;; transport-specific 4000 ended-stream close required by ws protocol.\n   324\t        (call-close! (:close-ended! (:config serving)) (:socket-handle session))\n   325\t        (remove-session! serving attachment))\n   326\t\n   327\t      (close-session! serving attachment))))\n   328\t\n   329\t\n   330\t(defn step!\n   331\t  \"Advance exactly one host-owned driver tick at `now`.\n   332\t\n   333\t   Ordering is intentional: transport acceptance/expiry first, then offer and\n   334\t   control observation, then each established attachment's traffic interpreter\n   335\t   and forwarder.  This function never waits, schedules itself, or installs a\n   336\t   callback; cadence is entirely the caller's policy.\"\n   337\t  [serving now]\n   338\t  (when (= :running (:lifecycle (state serving)))\n   339\t    (let [endpoint (:endpoint (state serving))\n   340\t          next-endpoint ((:endpoint-step (:config serving)) endpoint now)]\n   341\t      (when next-endpoint\n   342\t        (swap! (:state serving) assoc :endpoint next-endpoint)))\n   343\t    (poll-offers! serving)\n   344\t    (poll-control! serving)\n   345\t    ;; Snapshot keys: traffic/lifecycle handling can remove a session during\n   346\t    ;; this tick without changing which already-admitted sessions are advanced.\n   347\t    (doseq [attachment (keys (:sessions (state serving)))]\n   348\t      (when-let [session (get-in (state serving) [:sessions attachment])]\n   349\t        (poll-traffic! serving attachment session))\n   350\t      (when-let [session (get-in (state serving) [:sessions attachment])]\n   165\t;; =============================================================================\n   166\t\n   167\t#?(:cljd nil\n   168\t   :clj\n   169\t   (do\n   170\t     (defn- print-prompt!\n   171\t       []\n   172\t       (print prompt)\n   173\t       (flush))\n   174\t\n   175\t     (defn- drain-server!\n   176\t       \"The shell has quit, so the endpoint stops before the host exits: ask it\n   177\t        once, then keep stepping until it reports `:stopped` or the bounded\n   178\t        budget runs out.  A connected client must observe `:ws/ended`, not the\n   179\t        `:ws/closed` a process exit would leave behind.\"\n   180\t       [server]\n   181\t       (when server\n   182\t         (loop [server (serve/stop! server)\n   183\t                remaining stop-ticks]\n   184\t           (let [[server' lines stopped?] (stop-tick server\n   185\t                                                     (System/currentTimeMillis))]\n   186\t             (doseq [line lines]\n   187\t               (println line))\n   188\t             (cond\n   189\t               stopped? nil\n   190\t               (zero? remaining) (println stop-timeout-text)\n   191\t               :else (do (Thread/sleep ^long tick-millis)\n   192\t                         (recur server' (dec remaining))))))))\n   193\t\n   194\t     (defn poll-loop!\n   195\t       \"The sole owner of REPL and endpoint state on the JVM.  It carries both\n   196\t        values through serial steps; nothing is shared with the reader.\n   197\t\n   198\t        It also owns the exit.  The reader is parked in `read-line` and cannot\n   199\t        observe that the shell has quit, so a typed `(quit)` would otherwise\n   200\t        stop the endpoint and then wait for end-of-input that only Ctrl-D\n   201\t        produces.  `exit!` is called once, by this thread, after the endpoint\n   202\t        has drained.\"\n   203\t       ([state server headless?]\n   204\t        (poll-loop! state server headless? #(.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))\n   205\t       ([state server headless? exit!]\n   206\t        (loop [state state\n   207\t               server server]\n   208\t          (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n   209\t                                                 (System/currentTimeMillis))]\n   210\t            (doseq [line lines]\n   211\t              (println line))\n   212\t            (if (:running? state')\n   213\t              (do (when (and (seq lines) (not headless?))\n   214\t                    (print-prompt!))\n   215\t                  (Thread/sleep ^long tick-millis)\n   216\t                  (recur state' server'))\n   217\t              (do (drain-server! server')\n   218\t                  (println)\n   219\t                  (exit!)))))))\n   220\t\n   221\t     (defn- read-loop!\n   222\t       \"The reader parks in `read-line` and appends.  It reads no state.\"\n   223\t       [input]\n   224\t       (loop []\n   225\t         (if-let [line (read-line)]\n   226\t           (do (driver/submit-line! input line)\n   227\t               (recur))\n   228\t           (driver/submit-line! input \"(quit)\"))))\n   229\t\n   230\t     (defn -main\n   231\t       [& args]\n   232\t       (let [opts (parse-args args)\n   233\t             state (boot opts)\n   234\t             server (boot-server opts)\n   235\t             headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))]\n   236\t         (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n   237\t           (println line))\n   238\t         (let [poller (Thread. ^Runnable (fn [] (poll-loop! state server headless?)))]\n   239\t           (.setDaemon poller true)\n   240\t           (.start poller)\n   241\t           ;; Headless attends the endpoint only: there is no reader, so the\n   242\t           ;; step owner is joined until it stops, and the explicit stop trigger\n   243\t           ;; is the host signal a shutdown hook observes.  The hook appends a\n   244\t           ;; line like any producer and then waits for the one step owner.\n   245\t           (if headless?\n   246\t             (do (.addShutdownHook\n   247\t                   (Runtime/getRuntime)\n   248\t                   (Thread. ^Runnable (fn []\n   249\t                                        (request-stop! state)\n   250\t                                        (.join poller ^long stop-join-millis))))\n   251\t                 (.join poller))\n   252\t             (do (print-prompt!)\n   253\t                 ;; End-of-input is one way to stop; a typed `(quit)` is the\n   254\t                 ;; other, and the step owner has already exited the process by\n   255\t                 ;; the time this join is reached in that case.\n   256\t                 (read-loop! (:input state))\n   257\t                 (.join poller ^long stop-join-millis))))\n   258\t         (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))))\n   259\t\n   260\t\n   261\t;; =============================================================================\n   262\t;; cljs (Node) — a readline handler that only appends, and one interval owner\n   263\t;; =============================================================================\n   264\t\n   265\t#?(:cljs\n   266\t   (do\n   267\t     (defn- run-node!\n   268\t       [state server rl]\n   269\t       ;; `repl-step` is synchronous and the Node event loop is single threaded,\n   270\t       ;; so this interval callback cannot overlap itself.  The box is host\n   271\t       ;; cadence plumbing: the interval is the only reader and writer of it.\n   272\t       ;; `:stopping` is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards:\n   273\t       ;; the endpoint is asked to stop once and stepped until it reports it.\n   274\t       (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil})\n   275\t             timer (atom nil)\n   276\t             finish! (fn []\n   277\t                       (js/clearInterval @timer)\n   278\t                       (when rl (.close rl))\n   279\t                       (js/process.exit 0))]\n   280\t         (reset! timer\n   281\t                 (js/setInterval\n   282\t                   (fn []\n   283\t                     (let [{:keys [state server stopping]} @box]\n   284\t                       (if (nil? stopping)\n   285\t                         (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n   286\t                                                                (js/Date.now))]\n   287\t                           (reset! box {:state state' :server server' :stopping nil})\n   288\t                           (doseq [line lines]\n   289\t                             (js/console.log line))\n   290\t                           (cond\n   291\t                             (:running? state')\n   292\t                             (when (and (seq lines) rl) (.prompt rl))\n   293\t\n   294\t                             server'\n   295\t                             (swap! box assoc\n   296\t                                    :server (serve/stop! server')\n   297\t                                    :stopping stop-ticks)\n   298\t\n   299\t                             :else (finish!)))\n   300\t                         (let [[server' lines stopped?] (stop-tick server\n   301\t                                                                   (js/Date.now))]\n   302\t                           (doseq [line lines]\n   303\t                             (js/console.log line))\n   304\t                           (cond\n   305\t                             stopped? (finish!)\n   306\t                             (zero? stopping) (do (js/console.log stop-timeout-text)\n   307\t                                                  (finish!))\n   308\t                             :else (swap! box assoc\n   309\t                                          :server server'\n   310\t                                          :stopping (dec stopping)))))))\n   311\t                   tick-millis))))\n   312\t\n   313\t     (defn -main\n   314\t       [& args]\n   315\t       (let [opts (parse-args args)\n   316\t             state (boot opts)\n   317\t             server (boot-server opts)\n   318\t             readline (when-not (:headless? opts) (js/require \"readline\"))\n   319\t             rl (when readline\n   320\t                  (.createInterface readline\n   321\t                                    #js {:input (.-stdin js/process)\n   322\t                                         :output (.-stdout js/process)\n   323\t                                         :prompt prompt}))]\n   324\t         (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n   325\t           (js/console.log line))\n   326\t         (if rl\n   327\t           (do (.on rl \"line\" (fn [line] (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)))\n   328\t               (.on rl \"close\" (fn [] (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\")))\n   329\t               (.prompt rl))\n   330\t           ;; Headless has no reader, so the explicit stop trigger is the host\n   331\t           ;; signal: it appends a line and returns, like any producer.\n   332\t           (doseq [signal [\"SIGINT\" \"SIGTERM\"]]\n   333\t             (.on js/process signal (fn [] (request-stop! state)))))\n   334\t         (run-node! state server rl)))))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t;; =============================================================================\n   338\t;; cljd — a stdin listener that only appends, and one periodic timer owner\n   339\t;; =============================================================================\n   340\t\n   341\t#?(:cljd\n   342\t   (do\n   343\t     (defn- write-line!\n   344\t       [message]\n   345\t       (println message))\n   346\t\n   347\t     (defn- print-prompt!\n   348\t       []\n   349\t       (.write io/stdout prompt)\n   350\t       (.flush io/stdout))\n   351\t\n   352\t     (defn- run-dart!\n   353\t       \"One `Timer.periodic` owns both values.  A synchronous poll loop would\n   354\t        deadlock the Dart event loop: IO never progresses, so `blocked` never\n   355\t        clears.\n   356\t\n   357\t        `:stopping` is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards:\n   358\t        the endpoint is asked to stop once and stepped until it reports it, so\n   359\t        the host does not exit with a live listener.\"\n   360\t       [state server headless?]\n   361\t       (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil})]\n   362\t         (async/Timer.periodic\n   363\t           (dart-core/Duration .milliseconds tick-millis)\n   364\t           (fn [^async/Timer timer]\n   365\t             (let [{:keys [state server stopping]} @box\n   366\t                   now (.-millisecondsSinceEpoch (dart-core/DateTime.now))\n   367\t                   finish! (fn []\n   368\t                             (.cancel timer)\n   369\t                             (io/exit 0)\n   370\t                             nil)]\n   371\t               (if (nil? stopping)\n   372\t                 (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server now)]\n   373\t                   (reset! box {:state state' :server server' :stopping nil})\n   374\t                   (doseq [line lines]\n   375\t                     (write-line! line))\n   376\t                   (cond\n   377\t                     (:running? state')\n   378\t                     (when (and (seq lines) (not headless?))\n   379\t                       (print-prompt!))\n   380\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
