o.stream.rpc/poll!` — cadence is its caller's (the REPL driver, `dao.jing.remote`, `dao.jing.remote.step`) | `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:426` | Its session's one response medium; `blocked` answers `:dao.stream.rpc/idle` at once — the internal loop is only the `budget` drain | Single-stream | Nothing inside `poll!`: it is already the one-medium read step a waitset entry polls. Only its cadence callers could adopt a W3 driver |\n   205\t| `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` — no production caller in src; named only by the delegate `dao.stream.rpc/serve-once!` (`rpc.cljc:106`) | `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:301` | Request-medium `blocked`, then response-medium `full` (the pending response stays in state, so a retry cannot invoke the handler twice) | One session per call — two mediums, strictly sequential; not multiplexed | Nothing today: nothing in src loops over it. A host serving many sessions is what turns each session's request medium into a wait-set entry; that host, not this step, is the adoption site |\n   206\t| `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` — `yin.repl.core`'s evaluator stage (`core.cljc:543`), `yin.vm.macro`'s expander (`macro.cljc:1341`), `yin.vm.encoder` (`encoder.cljc:296`), `dao.space.index`'s walk sessions (`index.cljc:601-627`) | `src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:217` | Its one observer's medium; returns the session the moment `observe/step` answers `blocked` or `end`, looping only while the consumer is ready | Single-stream, one consumer | Nothing, as expected. Every caller coordinates exactly one medium per session — the correct single-stream shape W4 refuses to convert |\n   207\t| `dao.stream.serving`'s tick `step!` — driven by `yin.repl.serve/step` (`serve.cljc:701`) and `dao.jing.remote`'s daemon ticker (`remote.cljc:981-988`) | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:330` — `poll-offers!` `:260` (one reader per configured slot), `poll-control!` `:272`, per-session `poll-traffic!` `:286` and `advance-forwarder!` `:310`, the `forward-step` caller (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc:67`; its `:retry` covers both source `blocked` and destination `full`) | Every offer slot's reader, the control reader, and each established session's traffic reader and forwarder | Multiplexed (slots plus sessions, one pass per tick) | → W4 candidate. Each per-medium poll is exactly a wait-set entry — `:next` for the readers, `:put` for a destination-full forwarder; adoption deletes the tick's inline sweep and hands cadence to the W3 driver |\n   208\t| `dao.jing`'s intake materializer `observe-step!` — no production caller in src; driven by `test/dao/jing_test.cljc` | `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:505` | Every non-ended intake-pool member's stream, one round-robin pass, at most one payload materialized per call; blocked members never hide later members | Multiplexed (N pool members) | Nothing until a host composes a pool. When one does, the pool is the multi-waiter class W4's \"dao.jing if its materializer holds more than one waiter\" names — the structure is already a wait set in everything but name |\n   209\t| `dao.gui.event`'s pump `advance` — driven append-side by `datomworld.demo.voxel_input` (`voxel_input.cljc:235-238`, pumped after each append) and `datomworld.demo.artifact_runner` (`artifact_runner.cljc:168-184`, bounded, host-progress-driven) | `src/cljc/dao/gui/event.cljc:762` | The binding's single `:runtime-input` medium (`blocked` → `:blocked`) and its pending output writes (`full` → `:parked`, retried before the next read) | Single input stream per binding, plus bounded pending `:put` retries | The demos never idle-poll, so nothing burns CPU today, and `advance` is already one-medium-per-call. A waitset pays only if a composition holds several live bindings or arms idle cadence; W4's \"GUI event pump\" expectation resolves to these host drivers, not to `advance` itself |\n   210\t| `yin.repl`'s host shell — one tick owner per host: clj `poll-loop!` (`repl.cljc:194`, `Thread/sleep`), cljs `run-node!` (`repl.cljc:267`, `setInterval`), cljd `run-dart!` (`repl.cljc:352`, `Timer.periodic`) — all stepping `step-all` (`repl.cljc:93`) | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:21` (`tick-millis`, 25 ms); `repl-step` `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:536`, whose `poll-remote` (`:480`) → `adapter/poll-responses` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:202`) → `rpc/poll!`; `serve/step` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:684`) | Local input drain, the remote session's response medium, and the served endpoint's whole serving tick — every 25 ms whether or not anything moved | Multiplexed (one tick owner over all of it) | This is the fixed-timer cadence W3's driver replaces: the tick owner becomes a driver with `:poll-ms`/`:budget`, and the append-side readers (the per-host read-line handlers, `request-stop!`) become `nudge!` callers. The endpoint's multiplexing itself moves to `waitset/check` via the serving row above |\n   211\t\n   212\tLoops found beyond the expected rows:\n   213\t\n   214\t| Loop (caller) | Where | Waits on | Scope | What a waitset adoption changes |\n   215\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   216\t| `dao.jing.remote`'s `connect-content!` establishment wait — JVM-only host policy, named as such by `dao.stream.md` OD-5 | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:733-747`, stepping `await-established-step` (`:363`, one `rpc/poll!` at `:373`) per round, then `Thread/sleep` | The attachment's `/established` lifecycle element on one response medium, to a deadline | Single-stream | A driver plus waitset would replace the sleep-poll, and a nudge would end the sleep at establishment instead of at the next interval. Deliberately out of scope: this is the documented blocking host policy |\n   217\t| `dao.jing.remote`'s `call!` request wait | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:587-636`, stepping `call-step` (`:294`, `rpc/poll!` at `:308`) per round, then `Thread/sleep` under a deadline | One awaited call's completion; interruption is a first-class exit | Single-stream (one awaited call per thread) | As above — one sleeping JVM thread per in-flight call is precisely the idle cost the nudge halves |\n   218\t| `dao.postgraphics.terminal`'s viewer pump `step-until-blocked` — driven by the demo, web, and flutter hosts after renders or frames (`src/cljd/dao/postgraphics/flutter.cljd:339`, `src/cljs/dao/postgraphics/web.cljs:106`) | `src/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:169`, over `step` (`:125`, at most one frame read per call) | Its one frame medium; a bounded drain-until-blocked, after which the host retries at its next opportunity | Single-stream | Nothing should change. Same drain-on-progress shape as the GUI pump; no idle loop exists to replace |\n   219\t| One-shot catch-up drains: `dao.space.query/snapshot` and `datomworld.demo.continuation_transport` | `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:299`; `src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_transport.cljc:121,153` | Catch one cursor up to `blocked`/`end` once, for a relation value or a demo handoff | Single-stream | Nothing. A snapshot is a read that ends, not a waiter; there is no cadence across rounds to hand a driver |\n   220\t\n   221\tVerified to contain no loop of their own, so no row: `dao.stream.observe/step`\n   222\t(`observe.cljc:58`), `dao.stream.forward/forward-step` (`forward.cljc:67`),\n   223\t`dao.jing.remote.step`'s client (`step.cljc:492` composes `rpc/poll!` into a\n   224\tpure step), `yin.repl.connect/observe` (`connect.cljc:525`, one `stream/next`\n   225\tat `:542` per call, stepped by the shell's tick owner), and `dao.await`, which\n   226\tonly emits `:stream/next`/`:stream/put` effects (`await.cljc:66`) — its\n   227\twaiting is the engine row above. Every expected row was found in the source;\n   228\tnone was invented.\n   229\t\n   230\t## Phase W1 — The sweep\n   231\t\n   232\t`src/cljc/dao/stream/waitset.cljc`, pure, requiring only `dao.stream`:\n   233\t\n   234\t- `empty-waitset`, `park` (add an entry), `check` as specified above.\n   235\t- Tests iterate `dao.stream/outcomes-next` and `outcomes-append` and pin\n   236\t  the classification of every declared outcome — exactly `blocked` and\n   237\t  `full` wait — so a future contract outcome fails this suite instead of\n   238\t  falling into a default branch. A keyword outside the declared sets is\n   239\t  asserted terminal.\n   240\t- Tests for the two preserved properties: shared cursor-ref wakes\n   241\t  co-waiters on distinct values in one round; a scripted `full`-once\n   242\t  handle shows exactly one append per poll with the resolved writer in\n   243\t  `:woken` and no polled state discarded.\n   244\t\n   245\tDeliverable: suite green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd.\n   246\t\n   247\t## Phase W2 — The engine becomes a consumer\n   248\t\n   249\tReplace `augment-wait-entry`, `poll-wait-entry`, and `check-wait-set` in\n   250\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` with `waitset/check` plus a store-resolver\n   251\tand the woken-to-ready-queue disposition (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`\n   252\tstays the engine's). The run loop, park semantics, and terminal-outcome\n   253\traising are unchanged.\n   254\t\n   255\tDeliverable: the full `yin.vm` suite — engine, walker, FFI, macro, parity —\n   256\tgreen on all three hosts **without test edits**, and `grep` confirming the\n   257\tthree inline functions are gone. Any test that had to change is a defect in\n   258\tW1, not in the test.\n   259\t\n   260\t## Phase W3 — Cadence containers (`dao.stream.waitset.driver`)\n   261\t\n   262\tOne namespace name, three host files, the R2 shape with explicit state:\n   263\t`src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`,\n   264\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs`,\n   265\t`src/cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd`.\n   266\t\n   267\t- **clj**: `make-driver` returns `{:waitset … :queue (LinkedBlockingQueue.)\n   268\t  :poll-ms … :budget …}` held in an atom the host creates. `run-loop!`\n   269\t  blocks on the queue with the `:poll-ms` timeout — external entries and\n   270\t  `nudge!` wakes it immediately, timeout means the wait set is due a\n   335\tW1 and W2 are pure `.cljc` over `dao.stream` and have no host branch. W3 is\n   336\tthree host files by construction. Every phase green on clj, cljs (Node) and\n   337\tcljd; per the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.stream.waitset-test` and\n   338\t`Testing dao.stream.waitset.driver-test` appear in the Node output rather\n   339\tthan assuming discovery. The cljd test lane regenerates `test/cljd-out/`;\n   340\tonly one process may own it at a time.\n   341\t\n   342\t## Boundary of this plan\n   343\t\n   344\t**Not in this plan, by design:**\n   345\t\n   346\t- Any change to `dao.stream`, its protocols, outcome sets, or transports.\n   347\t  Zero diff is an end condition.\n   348\t- The contract's reserved readiness extension. The library is the\n   349\t  demonstration that polling plus a nudge suffices; if it ever is not,\n   350\t  the extension lands in transports and this library consumes it —\n   351\t  additively, per the contract.\n   352\t- `dao.await` and the VM's park/resume relation. W2 swaps the engine's\n   353\t  sweep implementation only.\n   354\t- Fairness, priorities, indexes (see *Decisions*), and any queue\n   355\t  interpreter restoring destructive take — an interpreter above the\n   356\t  stream, per the runtime plan's boundary, not a wait-set feature.\n   357\t- Consumers that stay single-stream on `observe/step`. They are not\n   358\t  technical debt; they are the correct shape for one stream and one\n   359\t  consumer.\n   360\t\n   361\t## End condition\n   362\t\n   363\tComplete when all of the following hold on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd:\n   364\t\n   365\t- `dao.stream.waitset` requires only `dao.stream`, classifies every\n   366\t  declared outcome with exactly `blocked`/`full` waiting, preserves the\n   367\t  shared-cursor and no-dropped-poll laws, and its suite proves each by\n   368\t  construction.\n   369\t- `yin.vm.engine` contains no inline wait-set functions and its suites\n   370\t  pass unmodified.\n   371\t- `dao.stream.waitset.driver` exists per host with explicit state,\n   372\t  configurable cadence, budget, and `nudge!`, over v2 fixtures only.\n   373\t- The W4 consumers run the library and contain no private multiplexed\n   374\t  poll loops; the single-stream consumers are recorded as deliberately\n   375\t  unchanged in W0's table.\n   376\t- `git diff <base> -- src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/`\n   377\t  shows no transport changes beyond the new `waitset/` subtree.\n   378\t- The W5 prose names the library, and no doc or docstring names the\n   379\t  deleted `dao.runtime` as a live namespace.\n    60\t  [options]\n    61\t  (let [budget (:batch-budget options)]\n    62\t    (if (and (integer? budget) (not (neg? budget)))\n    63\t      budget\n    64\t      0)))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn forward-step\n    68\t  \"Copy at most `:batch-budget` values from source to destination.\n    69\t\n    70\t  `source` must provide the DaoStream reader surface and `destination` the\n    71\t  writer surface. `state` is normally produced by `initial-state` or an earlier\n    72\t  call and contains `:cursor`. Options are ordinary data:\n    73\t\n    74\t  * `:batch-budget` is a non-negative integer. A zero budget performs no read.\n    75\t  * `:gap-policy` is `:resume` (continue from the gap result's recovery\n    76\t    cursor), `:terminate`, or a function receiving the gap result and returning\n    77\t    one of those keywords. A resume forwards no value, so it is bounded\n    78\t    separately: at most `:batch-budget` resumes happen in one call, after which\n    79\t    the step returns `:continue` at the last recovery cursor. A recovery cursor\n    80\t    equal to the cursor just read is a transport fixed point rather than\n    81\t    progress, and terminates the step as `:source-gap`.\n    82\t\n    83\t  The returned map always has `:cursor`, `:status`, and `:forwarded`.  It also\n    84\t  has `:outcome` whenever the last source/destination operation produced one.\n    85\t  `:continue` and `:retry` are non-terminal statuses. `:retry` is returned for\n    86\t  source `blocked` and destination `full`, and neither advances the cursor.\n    87\t  All other operation outcomes are terminal and are preserved as `:outcome`.\n    88\t  In particular, `end` only stops this interpreter; it never closes either\n    89\t  handle.\"\n    90\t  ([source destination state]\n    91\t   (forward-step source destination state default-options))\n    92\t  ([source destination state options]\n    93\t   (let [state (if (map? state) state {})\n    94\t         options (merge default-options (or options {}))\n    95\t         cursor (:cursor state)\n    96\t         status (:status state)]\n    97\t     ;; Terminal state is data owned by the driver. Re-stepping it is a no-op,\n    98\t     ;; which makes accidental extra driver ticks harmless and idempotent.\n    99\t     (if (and status (not (contains? #{:continue :retry} status)))\n   100\t       state\n   101\t       (loop [cursor cursor\n   102\t              remaining (positive-budget options)\n   103\t              resumes (positive-budget options)\n   104\t              forwarded 0]\n   105\t         (if (zero? remaining)\n   106\t           (result-state cursor :continue forwarded nil)\n   107\t           (let [observed (observe/step source\n   108\t                                        cursor\n   109\t                                        (fn [value]\n   110\t                                          (stream/append! destination value)))\n   111\t                 outcome (:outcome observed)]\n   112\t             (case (:status observed)\n   113\t               ;; The cursor advanced only because the append answered ok;\n   114\t               ;; `observe/step` owns that ordering.\n   115\t               :advance\n   116\t               (recur (:cursor observed) (dec remaining) resumes (inc forwarded))\n   117\t\n   118\t               ;; Source `blocked` and destination `full` are the same answer\n   119\t               ;; to this interpreter: no progress, cursor kept, step again.\n   120\t               :retry\n   121\t               (result-state cursor :retry forwarded outcome)\n   122\t\n   123\t               :gap\n   124\t               (let [recovery (:recovery observed)]\n   125\t                 (cond\n   126\t                   ;; A recovery cursor that does not move cannot be observed\n   127\t                   ;; from, whatever the policy asks for.  Terminating keeps the\n   128\t                   ;; step total against a defective transport.\n   129\t                   (or (not= :resume\n   130\t                             (gap-action (:gap-policy options) (:read observed)))\n   131\t                       (= recovery cursor))\n   132\t                   (result-state cursor :source-gap forwarded outcome)\n   133\t\n   134\t                   (pos? resumes)\n   135\t                   (recur recovery remaining (dec resumes) forwarded)\n   136\t\n   137\t                   ;; The resume allowance is spent.  Yield the recovered cursor\n   138\t                   ;; so the driver keeps this step's progress and decides when\n   139\t                   ;; to step again.\n   140\t                   :else (result-state recovery :continue forwarded nil)))\n   141\t\n   142\t               ;; A destination answer that is neither ok nor full.\n   143\t               :failed\n   144\t               (result-state cursor\n   145\t                             (terminal-status :destination outcome)\n   200\t  \"Rethrow `e`, keeping the throw and carrying the partial session in the\n   201\t   exception's data.\n   202\t\n   203\t   The original message and data are preserved and `:session` is added. The\n   204\t   rethrow is a new `ExceptionInfo` whose cause is the original, so a caller\n   205\t   matching the original exception's concrete type no longer matches the\n   206\t   wrapper — `(ex-cause caught)` names it. Recovery is opt-in: a caller that\n   207\t   does not read `:session` keeps the old semantics — a throw means the\n   208\t   round failed — and a caller that catches resumes from the carried\n   209\t   session, which names the furthest progress the round had made where the\n   210\t   failure happened.\"\n   211\t  [session e]\n   212\t  (throw (ex-info (or (ex-message e) (str e))\n   213\t                  (assoc (ex-data e) :session session)\n   214\t                  e)))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn run-on-stream\n   218\t  \"Coordinate one `{:observer observer :consumer consumer}` session over the\n   219\t   attached medium, returning the updated session.\n   220\t\n   221\t   The consumer is any value the three supplied functions agree on. `ready?`\n   222\t   decides when it may accept another batch, `load` hands it one observed\n   223\t   batch and returns the successor consumer, and `run` lets it do whatever\n   224\t   work a loaded batch implies — evaluate, expand, index, forward — and\n   225\t   returns the successor. This is coordination, not interpretation: no field\n   226\t   of the consumer is inspected, so any consumer shape can be driven.\n   227\t\n   228\t   A consumer that is not ready runs first, and observation follows only if\n   229\t   it becomes ready. A ready consumer observes one batch; `gap` commits the\n   230\t   recovery cursor and continues; `blocked` and `end` return the session.\n   231\t   After a batch loads and runs, a consumer that is ready again observes the\n   232\t   next batch, and one that is not returns the session without another read.\n   233\t\n   234\t   A `load` or `run` that throws still propagates — the throw is kept — but\n   235\t   carries the partial session in the exception's data under `:session`, so\n   236\t   recovery is opt-in. Where the failure happened decides the carried\n   237\t   session: a throwing `load` carries the cursor *before* the failing batch,\n   238\t   so a retry re-reads it; a throwing `run` carries the cursor *after* the\n   239\t   loaded batch and the consumer the failing `run` left — the partial state\n   240\t   it reported under `:consumer` in its own exception's data when it made\n   241\t   progress before throwing, else the state `load` returned — so a retry\n   242\t   neither re-reads the batch nor repeats work already delivered; a\n   243\t   terminal read after processed batches carries the session those batches\n   244\t   produced. A consumer whose input can be bad should therefore return that\n   245\t   as data from `load` rather than throw, and reserve the throw for its own\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '295,338p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc | sed -n '100,150p'; rg -n 'defn- sync-sessions|sync-sessions' src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '295,338p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc | sed -n '100,150p'; rg -n 'defn- sync-sessions|sync-sessions' src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   295\tholds more than one waiter. Single-stream consumers stay on `observe/step`\n   296\tand `observer/run-on-stream`: the waitset is for hosts holding many\n   297\twaiters, and converting a one-stream loop onto it is the over-adoption\n   298\tfailure mode this phase exists to avoid. Each adoption deletes the loop it\n   299\treplaces; a consumer adopted without deleting anything has been wrapped,\n   300\tnot migrated, and is reverted.\n   301\t\n   302\tDeliverable: each adopted consumer's suite green on all three hosts with\n   303\tits private poll loop gone.\n   304\t\n   305\t## Phase W5 — Design prose and stale references\n   306\t\n   307\t- The `dao.stream.observe` namespace docstring's reference to\n   308\t  \"`dao.runtime`'s wait set\" names `dao.stream.waitset`.\n   309\t- [`dao.await.md`](./dao.await.md) describes resumption as \"the scheduler\n   310\t  polls each parked entry\" — the sentence gains the library's name and the\n   311\t  nudge rule (the VM parks; the library sweeps; the driver keeps cadence).\n   312\t- [`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) *The readiness extension* gains one\n   313\t  sentence pointing at this library as the standing answer to *What it\n   314\t  costs*: poll-based, substrate-untouched, and the reason no consumer\n   315\t  needs the reserved extension to sleep between rounds.\n   316\t\n   317\tDeliverable: the three docs updated; no other prose touched.\n   318\t\n   319\t## Divergence register\n   320\t\n   321\tEvery place the library deliberately differs from what it replaces, with\n   322\tthe reason.\n   323\t\n   324\t| Prior form | `dao.stream.waitset` | Why |\n   325\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   326\t| Engine entries carry VM registers and ride the machine relation | Entries are `{:reason :stream-ref :cursor-ref}` data; disposition is host-owned | A VM entry is a continuation; the library multiplexes readiness. Register-bearing entries would drag the VM into every consumer |\n   327\t| Engine resolves refs against its `:store` internally (`engine.cljc:266`) | Resolver is a parameter; `check` returns a new store | The deferred decision, adopted: a second consumer class appeared (W0) |\n   328\t| `dao.runtime` had `:resume`, a ready queue, and task semantics | None of the three | Task scheduling is why the old runtime was coupled to its consumers; readiness multiplexing is the reusable core. A library with `:resume` is a scheduler, and schedulers are per-host policy |\n   329\t| Deleted R2 drivers polled on a fixed timer only | `nudge!` wakes an idle driver immediately | The timer-only shape pays full `:poll-ms` latency on externally-appended data; the nudge halves the cost of the select analogy without touching the substrate |\n   330\t| `run-once`/`run-loop` combined ready work and polling | `check` polls; the host's disposition runs woken work | The R1 split, restated: a polled state is never discarded, and the sweep owns no downstream work to drop |\n   331\t| Unknown wait `:reason` kept waiting (old runtime default) | Unchanged | An entry the library cannot poll is not the library's to resolve; it stays where the host put it |\n   332\t\n   333\t## Host matrix\n   334\t\n   335\tW1 and W2 are pure `.cljc` over `dao.stream` and have no host branch. W3 is\n   336\tthree host files by construction. Every phase green on clj, cljs (Node) and\n   337\tcljd; per the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.stream.waitset-test` and\n   338\t`Testing dao.stream.waitset.driver-test` appear in the Node output rather\n   100\t  [capacity]\n   101\t  (:dao.stream/handle\n   102\t    (ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n   103\t                   ring/capacity-key capacity})))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- mint\n   107\t  [handle anchor]\n   108\t  (let [result (stream/cursor handle anchor)]\n   109\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   110\t      (:dao.stream/cursor result))))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- writer-target\n   114\t  [handle]\n   115\t  {:dao.stream/handle handle :dao.stream/surface #{:writer}})\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn- publish\n   119\t  [endpoint kind text]\n   120\t  (update endpoint :outbox conj {outbox-event-key kind text-key text}))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn take-outbox\n   124\t  \"Return `[entries next-endpoint]`, clearing the publication outbox once.\"\n   125\t  [endpoint]\n   126\t  [(:outbox endpoint) (assoc endpoint :outbox [])])\n   127\t\n   128\t\n   129\t(defn- deposit-fn\n   130\t  [lifecycle]\n   131\t  (fn deposit!\n   132\t    [kind value]\n   133\t    (stream/append! lifecycle {event-key kind value-key value})))\n   134\t\n   135\t\n   136\t;; =============================================================================\n   137\t;; serve!\n   138\t;; =============================================================================\n   139\t\n   140\t(defn- make-slots\n   141\t  [n]\n   142\t  (mapv (fn [_]\n   143\t          (let [offer (buffer 1)\n   144\t                ack (buffer 1)]\n   145\t            {:offer offer :ack ack\n   146\t             :offer-cursor (mint offer stream/anchor-newest)\n   147\t             :ack-cursor (mint ack stream/anchor-newest)}))\n   148\t        (range n)))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n443:(defn- sync-sessions\n703:              sync-sessions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
